[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]VRAM RipperDoc - Performance Optimizer[/color][/b][/size]
[size=3]Stuttering with a lot of texture or clothing mods? This shows you [b]which mods use up your graphics card's memory (VRAM)[/b], and can shrink just the heaviest textures with one double-click. Your mods are never changed.[/size]

[size=4][b]Who it's for[/b][/size]
Anyone with lots of texture, clothing, character, vehicle or environment mods. It matters most on [b]8–16 GB cards[/b], but big 4K texture packs can fill even a [b]24 GB[/b] card. When VRAM runs out you get stutter, blurry textures popping in, or crashes. If you're not short on VRAM this won't raise your FPS, but the report still finds broken and useless files.

[size=4][b]Quick start (3 double-clicks)[/b][/size]
After installing, open [i]Cyberpunk 2077\tools\VRAM RipperDoc\[/i] and double-click:
[list=1]
[*][b]1 - Scan my mods.bat[/b]: checks every texture and mesh mod and opens a report in your browser. Changes nothing. With a big mod list this takes a few minutes; a progress counter shows how far it is. Using Mod Organizer 2? If no mods are found, it asks for your MO2 folder once and remembers it.
[*][b]2 - Apply budget fix.bat[/b] [i](optional)[/i]: if the report says you're over budget, this adds [b]one extra file[/b] with half-size copies of only your heaviest textures. It scans first, then shows how many textures it would shrink, how big the file will be and roughly how long it takes, and asks before writing anything. A second question covers the 4K textures of new clothing, cars and props. A "Writing fix: X / Y" counter shows progress, and when it's done it says what it did and shows the new file in Explorer. Your mods stay exactly as they are.
[*][b]3 - Measure VRAM in game.bat[/b] [i](optional)[/i]: records how much VRAM the game really uses while you play. Run it once named "before" and once named "after" (same save, same spot) to see what changed.
[/list]
Changed your mind? Double-click [b]Undo budget fix.bat[/b] and everything is back as it was.

[size=4][b]What the report tells you[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Which mods are heaviest[/b]: how much extra VRAM each mod adds compared to the normal game, biggest first
[*][b]Why[/b]: oversized textures (4K/8K), very detailed models that never simplify at a distance, textures saved in wasteful formats
[*][b]Broken or missing[/b]: corrupt downloads, and mods that need another mod you haven't installed (a common cause of crashes and invisible items)
[*][b]Wasted files[/b]: files that never load because another mod overrides them, files identical to the normal game, and duplicates across mods
[*][b]Which installed mod each file belongs to[/b] (works with Vortex and Mod Organizer 2), with a link to its Nexus page
[*][b]Your budget[/b]: how much VRAM your mods can use on your graphics card at your in-game settings
[/list]

[size=4][b]Reading the report[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Each mod gets two numbers. [b]Normal play[/b] is the extra VRAM it adds because it replaces game files, so it's loaded whenever those things are on screen; this is what counts against your budget. [b]When used[/b] is for new clothing, cars, weapons and props, which only take VRAM while you wear, drive or spawn them.
[*]Mods at the top of the table cost the most during normal play. Click a mod to see its heaviest files.
[*][color=#ff4d6d]Red numbers[/color] are worth a look: flags, missing files, or being over budget.
[*]Over budget? The simplest fix is often the [b]1K or 2K version[/b] of a big texture mod, if the author offers one. Otherwise use [b]2 - Apply budget fix[/b].
[*]Need help with a crash or stutter? Click [b]Copy summary for a support post[/b] and paste it into your post.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Install[/b][/size]
Install with [b]Vortex[/b] or [b]Mod Organizer 2[/b] like any other mod, or extract the zip into your Cyberpunk 2077 folder (the one with [i]bin[/i] and [i]archive[/i]).
No requirements. It runs on its own (Windows 10/11, 64-bit).

[size=4][b]FAQ[/b][/size]
[spoiler]
[b]Is it safe? Does it change my mods?[/b]
Scanning only reads files. The optional budget fix adds one new file ([i]archive\pc\mod\!RipperDoc_Budget.archive[/i]) and never edits your mods. Delete that file, or run Undo budget fix, to go back.

[b]Will textures look worse?[/b]
Only the textures the fix shrinks, and only to half size. That's the same smaller version the game already shows from a distance. Everything else stays sharp. If something looks too soft up close, undo the fix.

[b]How much VRAM will I save?[/b]
It depends on your mods and what's on screen. The report estimates the maximum; [b]3 - Measure VRAM in game[/b] shows the real number. In our test with [i]no texture mods at all[/i] (the worst case for this tool), halving 360 of the game's own textures saved about 120 MB at one spot in the Badlands. Heavy texture modlists have far more to trim.

[b]How do I read "Normal play" and "When used"?[/b]
[b]Normal play[/b] is what the mod costs whenever the things it replaces are on screen. [b]When used[/b] comes [i]on top of that[/i], only while you wear, drive or spawn the mod's new items. Example: a mod showing 11 MB normal play / 1.09 GB when used costs 11 MB most of the time, and up to about 1.1 GB more while its new items are in use. A [b]negative[/b] Normal play number means the mod makes the game [i]lighter[/i] than vanilla, for example with smaller textures or by removing objects from the world.

[b]My "When used" total is huge (hundreds of GB). Is my game about to explode?[/b]
No. That's every piece of clothing, every car and every prop you have installed, added up as if you used all of them at the same moment, which never happens. The game only loads the ones you're wearing, driving or looking at. That's why only [b]Normal play[/b] counts against your budget.

[b]Where is the fix file? I can't find it.[/b]
After [b]2 - Apply budget fix[/b] finishes, it opens Explorer with the new file selected: [i]archive\pc\mod\!RipperDoc_Budget.archive[/i]. If it says "Nothing to fix", your mods already fit (answer Y to the second question to shrink new items' 4K textures anyway). [b]Mod Organizer 2 users:[/b] when you run it through MO2, the fix is saved as a new MO2 mod called [i]VRAM RipperDoc Budget Fix[/i]. Press F5 (Refresh) in MO2 and tick it to enable it.

[b]The scan or the fix seems stuck / nothing opens.[/b]
Big mod lists (thousands of archives) take a few minutes. Watch the "Scanning mods: X / Y" counter, and while the fix is being written, the "Writing fix: X / Y textures" counter with time left. The report opens in your browser when it's done, and is also saved as [i]VRAM-RipperDoc-Report.html[/i] next to the tool.
[b]Please use v0.2.6 or newer.[/b] Versions 0.2.3 to 0.2.5 could hang forever right after "all textures done" if one of your mod archives has a space (or similar character) right after its leading "!" or starts with a space, e.g. "! Hairs.archive". The tool was stuck choosing the fix file's name. That's fixed. Through Mod Organizer 2 the fix is saved as a normal MO2 mod (see above), and clicking inside the black window no longer pauses it. If it ever stops moving for a minute, it says so. Please post the [i]VRAM-RipperDoc-Fix.log[/i] file from the tool's folder in the comments so I can fix it.
A leftover [i]RipperDoc-....ripperdoc-tmp[/i] (or older [i].part[/i]) file is an unfinished fix. Running the fix or Undo again deletes it.

[b]Does it work with Mod Organizer 2?[/b]
Yes. It looks for your MO2 setup automatically. If no mods are found, [b]1 - Scan my mods[/b] asks for your MO2 folder (the one with ModOrganizer.ini) once and remembers it. You can also run it from inside MO2 as an executable. When run through MO2, [b]2 - Apply budget fix[/b] saves the fix as an MO2 mod named [i]VRAM RipperDoc Budget Fix[/i]: refresh MO2 (F5) and enable it. Undo budget fix deletes it again.

[b]Why does a car or mirror mod use so much?[/b]
The numbers are the mod's own textures and 3D models. Vehicle mods often ship 4K textures for every part, which adds up fast. Click the mod in the report to see exactly which files are heavy.

[b]Will it raise my FPS?[/b]
Only if you're running out of VRAM, which shows up as stutter and textures loading late. If you have plenty to spare, it won't change your FPS.

[b]I updated my mods. Do I need to redo the fix?[/b]
Run 2 - Apply budget fix again; it replaces the old fix file. The report also shows what changed since your last scan.
[/spoiler]

[size=4][b]How it's different from other performance mods[/b][/size]
[spoiler]
[list]
[*][b]Ultra+, OAI Tweaks, PinkStone[/b] tune the game engine. RipperDoc shows which of your [i]mods[/i] use up the VRAM those settings have to work with.
[*][b]FrameFlow, Adaptive Graphics Quality[/b] lower quality after your FPS drops. RipperDoc finds the cause and fixes just that.
[*][b]RedProfiler, G's TOTAL Profiler[/b] measure [i]script[/i] mods. RipperDoc does the same for [i]texture and model[/i] mods.
[*][b]Universal Texture Downscaler[/b] shrinks [i]every[/i] texture. RipperDoc shrinks only the few heaviest ones, so everything else stays sharp.
[*][b]CP2077 Texture Merger[/b] picks the better texture when mods clash. RipperDoc shows what each one costs, and which copies never load.
[*][b]Advanced Crash Reporter[/b] can only say "an archive mod may be responsible". RipperDoc names corrupt archives and missing requirements.
[/list]
Use it [i]alongside[/i] any of these.
[/spoiler]

[size=4][b]Technical details (for modders)[/b][/size]
[spoiler]
[list]
[*]Reads the game's load order (archive\pc\mod in ASCII order or modlist.txt, then REDmod, plus MO2 mods outside the game folder). The first archive wins a conflict.
[*]Parses RDAR archives and CR2W headers with the game's own Oodle DLL. Texture cost comes from the rendRenderTextureBlobPC buffer size, mesh triangles and LODs from rendRenderMeshBlob chunk infos.
[*]Compares against the vanilla content and ep1 archives (about 550k files), so costs are deltas and only references the mod [i]added[/i] can count as missing.
[*]"Normal play" = files that override vanilla plus new files reachable from them through references; everything else counts as "when used".
[*]Flags: 4K/8K textures, no mipmaps, uncompressed textures, N× vanilla pixels or triangles, no LODs, dense meshes, missing hard imports, identical-to-vanilla files, duplicates, likely unused files.
[*]The budget fix drops the top mip of each planned texture (no re-encoding). It rewrites sizes, offsets and every checksum, and writes a sorted, 4 KiB-aligned archive. Tested in game on 2.5.
[*]The live monitor reads Windows "GPU Process Memory" performance counters, the same source as Task Manager.
[*]Command line: [i]VramRipperdoc.exe --help[/i] (--budget, --include-new-items, --json, --hashes, --share, --community and more).
[/list]
Source code (MIT): [url=https://github.com/La2o/Cyberpunk2077-VRAMRipperDoc-PerformanceOptimizer]GitHub[/url]
[/spoiler]

[b]Help other players (optional):[/b] run [i]VramRipperdoc.exe --share[/i] to create an anonymous file with only mod file names and their VRAM numbers, and submit it on GitHub. Over time this builds a list of how heavy popular mods are.
